CAT = "cisaProtection"; SEG = "PRO"
Q = [
("What is the best test of whether leavers' system access is removed?",
 "Match HR leaver lists to active accounts", ["Ask IT whether it removes access promptly", "Read the access management policy", "Count the accounts created this year"],
 "Comparing people who left with accounts still enabled gives direct evidence. Inquiry or reading the policy shows only what should happen."),
("Managers approve every user in each access review without a single change, year after year. What should the IS auditor suspect?",
 "The reviews are rubber-stamped", ["Access is managed without fault", "The review tool runs too slowly", "Users hold too few permissions"],
 "In a changing workforce some access should be removed each cycle. Reviews with no changes suggest reviewers are not checking, so the auditor tests a sample of approvals."),
("Administrators share an emergency 'firecall' account on critical servers. Which control gives the best accountability?",
 "Vault checkout with a logged reason", ["A password changed once a year", "A list kept of everyone who knows the password", "A rule that only seniors use it"],
 "A privileged access vault releases the password to one named person at a time, records why and when, and can rotate it after use, so each use is traceable."),
("New network switches are being installed. What should the IS auditor check first?",
 "Default passwords have been changed", ["The switches came from one supplier", "The switches are the newest model", "Spare switches are kept in stock"],
 "Vendor default credentials are published and widely known, so leaving them in place gives anyone an easy way in."),
("What is the most important control when enrolling staff in a biometric access system?",
 "Verifying identity before enrollment", ["Using the reader with the lowest error rate", "Enrolling everyone on the same day", "Letting staff enroll themselves"],
 "A biometric only proves the person matches the template. If the wrong person is enrolled under a name, every later match is wrong too."),
("Accounts lock after a few failed login attempts. Which attack does this mainly slow down?",
 "Online password guessing", ["Phishing for credentials", "Theft of password hashes", "Session cookie hijacking"],
 "Lockout limits how many guesses an attacker can make against a live login. It does nothing against stolen hashes cracked offline or credentials handed over to a phishing page."),
("An IS auditor wants to find users who can both create vendors and approve payments in an ERP system. What is the best approach?",
 "Run a rule-based conflict analysis", ["Interview a few finance managers", "Read the job descriptions held on file", "Review last year's audit report"],
 "Analysing all users' actual permissions against a matrix of incompatible duties finds every conflict, not just the ones people remember."),
("Badge records show who entered the data center. How should they be used as a control?",
 "Compare entries with the authorized list", ["Keep them unread unless there is a theft", "Delete them at the end of each week", "Share them with all staff each month"],
 "Regular review of entries against those with approved access finds badge sharing, unauthorized entry and access that should be removed."),
("A server room's cooling failed overnight and equipment overheated before anyone noticed. Which control was missing?",
 "Temperature alarms to on-call staff", ["A larger cooling unit in the room", "A weekly check of the thermostat", "A sign asking staff to shut the door"],
 "Environmental sensors that alert someone at any hour let staff act before damage occurs. A bigger unit can still fail, and weekly checks would not catch an overnight fault."),
("Where are water detectors most useful in a data center with a raised floor?",
 "Under the raised floor", ["On the ceiling tiles", "In the reception area", "On each server's front panel"],
 "Leaks from cooling pipes or water ingress collect unseen under the floor, near cables and power, so sensors there give early warning."),
("Why are clean agent gas systems often chosen to protect computer rooms?",
 "They leave no residue on equipment", ["They are cheaper than any sprinkler", "They need no maintenance at all", "They also cool the room's air"],
 "Clean agents put out fire without water or powder, so electronics are not damaged by the suppressant. Rooms still need evacuation procedures."),
("Where should an emergency power-off switch in a data center be placed?",
 "Near exits, behind a protective cover", ["Next to the main server rack", "In the facility manager's locked office", "Outside the building's main door"],
 "Staff must reach it quickly while leaving, but a cover stops it being pressed by accident and shutting everything down."),
("What is the main purpose of an uninterruptible power supply in a data center?",
 "To bridge power until the generator runs", ["To replace the backup generator entirely", "To cut the building's power bill each month", "To cool servers during a fire"],
 "A UPS supplies clean power for minutes, covering short outages and the time a generator needs to start. Long outages depend on the generator."),
("Which location is generally best for a data center within a multistory building?",
 "A middle floor", ["The basement", "The top floor", "The ground floor beside the street"],
 "Middle floors avoid flooding risks in basements and roof leaks or easier access from above, and are harder to reach from the street."),
("What should an IS auditor look for first in a firewall rule base review?",
 "Overly broad or unused rules", ["The vendor's latest firmware version", "The number of administrators", "The age of the firewall hardware"],
 "Rules that allow any source or any service, or that no longer serve a purpose, open paths attackers can use. Each rule should trace to an approved need."),
("A retailer separates its card payment systems from the rest of the network. What is the main benefit?",
 "It limits exposure and compliance scope", ["It removes the need for encryption of card data", "It speeds up all card payments", "It lets staff use shared logins"],
 "Segmentation keeps card data in a smaller, well-controlled zone, reducing the number of systems an attacker can reach and that must meet card industry rules."),
("An intrusion prevention system is switched to blocking mode before it is tuned. What is the main risk?",
 "Legitimate traffic gets blocked", ["Attacks are logged twice", "The IPS stops logging attacks it sees", "Log files grow too small"],
 "An untuned IPS produces false positives, and in blocking mode those stop real business traffic. Tuning in detection mode first reduces that risk."),
("Remote staff use a VPN with split tunnelling turned on. What is the main concern?",
 "Internet traffic skips corporate controls", ["The VPN becomes slower for every user", "Users cannot reach internal systems at all", "The VPN stops encrypting traffic"],
 "With split tunnelling, a device talks to the internet directly while connected to the corporate network, bypassing web filtering and monitoring."),
("How can an organization best find unauthorized wireless access points on its premises?",
 "Regular wireless scanning", ["Asking staff to report them", "Changing the Wi-Fi name", "Turning off guest Wi-Fi"],
 "Rogue access points can be set up by staff or attackers and bypass network controls. Scheduled or continuous wireless scans detect them."),
("Laptops holding customer data are often lost. Which control best protects the data?",
 "Full-disk encryption", ["A cable lock for each laptop", "An asset tag on the case", "A strong BIOS password"],
 "Encryption keeps data unreadable on a lost device. A BIOS password can be bypassed by removing the drive."),
("Staff read work email on their own phones. Which capability matters most if a phone is lost?",
 "Remote wipe of work data", ["A larger phone screen", "Faster mobile data plans", "A phone case with a logo"],
 "Mobile device management lets the organization wipe the work container or the device remotely, so lost phones do not expose company data."),
("Smart building sensors are being connected to the network. What should be done?",
 "Put them on a separate segment", ["Give them internet-facing admin pages", "Connect them to the finance network", "Leave their factory settings as they are"],
 "IoT devices are often hard to patch and weakly secured. Isolating them and changing default settings limits the damage if one is compromised."),
("A new data loss prevention tool is being rolled out. How should it usually start?",
 "In monitor-only mode to tune rules", ["Blocking all email attachments at once", "Only on the IT team's own devices", "With every rule turned off"],
 "Watching traffic first shows how data really moves and which rules cause false alarms, so blocking can be turned on without disrupting the business."),
("Where should the keys used to encrypt a customer database be stored?",
 "Separately from the encrypted data", ["In the same table as the data", "In the application's configuration file", "On the database server's desktop"],
 "If keys sit next to the data, anyone who steals the data gets the keys too. A key management system or hardware security module keeps them apart."),
("A web server does not check certificate revocation status. What is the main risk?",
 "Revoked certificates may be trusted", ["Certificates expire too quickly", "Encryption stops working entirely", "The server rejects all certificates"],
 "A certificate revoked after compromise looks valid unless revocation is checked, so an attacker holding its key could be trusted."),
("Which security service does a digital signature provide that a shared-key message authentication code does not?",
 "Non-repudiation", ["Confidentiality", "Availability", "Compression"],
 "Only the signer holds the private key, so the signer cannot credibly deny signing. With a shared key, either party could have produced the code."),
("How can staff confirm that a downloaded software file has not been altered?",
 "Compare its hash with the published one", ["Check the file's name and size match", "Open it to see if it runs", "Ask a colleague if it looks right"],
 "A cryptographic hash changes if even one bit changes. Matching it with the value the vendor publishes over a trusted channel shows integrity."),
("In a SaaS arrangement, which task normally stays with the customer?",
 "Granting and removing user access", ["Patching the application code", "Maintaining the physical server hardware", "Running the data center's power"],
 "Under the shared responsibility model, the provider runs the platform and application, but the customer decides who can use the service and what data goes in."),
("An organization runs servers on an infrastructure-as-a-service platform. Who patches the guest operating systems?",
 "The customer", ["The cloud provider", "The hardware maker", "The internet provider"],
 "With IaaS the provider secures the physical and virtualization layers; everything from the operating system up is the customer's job."),
("What should come first in a program to protect sensitive data?",
 "Find and classify the data", ["Buy and deploy an encryption product", "Block all removable media", "Write a data breach response plan"],
 "Data cannot be protected in proportion to its value until the organization knows where it is and how sensitive it is."),
("Records reach the end of their retention period, but a lawsuit is expected. What should happen?",
 "Keep them under a legal hold", ["Delete them as scheduled", "Move them to staff laptops", "Print them and shred the files"],
 "A legal hold suspends normal deletion so evidence is preserved. Destroying relevant records once litigation is expected can bring serious penalties."),
("Which approach to security awareness training is most effective?",
 "Ongoing training fitted to each role", ["One long course when staff join", "A poster in each break room", "An annual email with the full policy"],
 "Regular, role-based training keeps risks fresh and relevant. A single session at hiring is soon forgotten."),
("A programmer adds code that moves fractions of a cent from many accounts into one account. What is this attack called?",
 "The salami technique", ["A logic bomb", "Data diddling", "A trapdoor"],
 "Salami slicing takes tiny amounts from many transactions, small enough to escape notice individually."),
("A clerk changes figures on source documents before they are keyed into the system. What is this called?",
 "Data diddling", ["Piggybacking", "Session hijacking", "IP spoofing"],
 "Data diddling alters data before or during input. Input controls and independent checks of source documents help detect it."),
("A program is written to delete payroll files if its author's name is ever removed from the payroll. What is this?",
 "A logic bomb", ["A self-copying worm", "Spyware", "A rootkit"],
 "A logic bomb lies dormant until a set condition occurs. Code review and change control are the main defences."),
("Someone drives around an office park scanning for wireless networks to break into. What is this called?",
 "War driving", ["Shoulder surfing", "Dumpster diving", "Vishing"],
 "War driving looks for wireless networks from a moving vehicle, often to find weakly secured ones."),
("A caller claiming to be from the IT help desk asks a user for her password to fix an urgent problem. What is this?",
 "Pretexting", ["Tailgating", "Baiting", "Typosquatting"],
 "Pretexting uses an invented story to gain trust and extract information. Users should know IT staff never ask for passwords."),
("A finance clerk receives a call in the voice of the CEO asking for an urgent transfer. What is the best control?",
 "Call back on a known number to confirm", ["Transfer at once if the voice matches", "Ask the caller for their employee number", "Reply by email to the caller's address"],
 "Voices can be cloned with AI. Verifying through a separate, known channel before paying defeats impersonation."),
("Which control is most effective against cross-site scripting?",
 "Encoding output before display", ["Longer session timeout periods", "Stronger TLS cipher settings", "Stronger disk encryption on servers"],
 "Encoding user-supplied data before it is shown in a page stops the browser from running it as script, along with input validation."),
("A company starts a public bug bounty program. What must it define first?",
 "Scope and safe harbor rules", ["The size of its marketing budget", "Which staff may file bugs", "The color of its web site"],
 "Researchers need to know which systems they may test and that good-faith testing will not be prosecuted, while the company sets limits that protect production."),
("A SIEM raises thousands of alerts a day, nearly all false. What should be done?",
 "Tune the correlation rules", ["Turn the SIEM off", "Hire staff to read every alert", "Stop collecting logs"],
 "Alert fatigue means real attacks get missed. Tuning rules to the environment cuts noise so analysts can act on what matters."),
("Ransomware is found encrypting files on two workstations. What should the response team do first?",
 "Isolate the affected machines", ["Pay the ransom right away", "Restore every server from backup", "Email all staff the ransom note"],
 "Containment stops the spread to more systems. Eradication and recovery follow once the scope is known."),
("When should an organization's incident response plan be tested?",
 "Regularly, before a real incident", ["Only after a breach has occurred", "Once, when it is first written", "When auditors ask to see it"],
 "Exercises reveal gaps in roles, contacts and tools while there is time to fix them, so the plan works under pressure."),
("What is the main purpose of a lessons-learned review after an incident?",
 "To improve controls and response", ["To assign blame to the people involved", "To close the incident ticket quickly", "To issue a press release"],
 "The review looks at what happened, what worked and what did not, and feeds changes into controls and the response plan."),
("How should an investigator copy a suspect's hard drive for forensic analysis?",
 "A bit-stream image via a write blocker", ["Copy all the files to a USB stick", "Boot the drive and run a normal backup", "Email the files to the analyst"],
 "A bit-stream image captures everything, including deleted data, and a write blocker stops the original from being changed."),
("After imaging a drive, how does the investigator show the copy is exact?",
 "Matching hashes of image and original", ["Comparing the two drives' file counts", "Checking the image's file creation date", "Asking the owner to confirm"],
 "Identical hash values prove the image is a true copy. Analysis is then done on the copy, keeping the original as evidence."),
("Personal data of customers has been exposed in a breach. Who should be involved early in deciding on notification?",
 "Legal counsel", ["The marketing team", "The external auditor", "The help desk staff"],
 "Notification duties and deadlines depend on the laws that apply, so legal advice is needed early to meet them."),
("Management says its ISO/IEC 27001 certificate means the IS auditor need not test controls over a new payments system. What should the auditor do first?",
 "Check what the certificate's scope covers", ["Accept the certificate and skip the work", "Ask the certification body for its fees", "Repeat the full certification audit"],
 "A certificate covers only the scope of the management system that was assessed, at that time. The auditor checks whether the new system falls inside it before deciding how far to rely on it."),
("Under ISO/IEC 27001, which document lists the controls an organization chose and why?",
 "The Statement of Applicability", ["The risk treatment plan summary", "The audit committee charter", "The business continuity plan"],
 "The Statement of Applicability records which reference controls apply, whether they are in place and the reason for any exclusion."),
("Who should approve the organization's information security policy?",
 "Senior management or the board", ["The security operations team lead", "The chief information officer alone", "The software vendor"],
 "Approval at the top shows the policy reflects management's intent and gives it authority across the organization."),
("How should an application store user passwords?",
 "As salted hashes", ["In plain text", "As base64 text", "As reversible codes"],
 "A salted hash cannot be reversed, and the salt stops identical passwords producing identical hashes, which defeats precomputed tables."),
("Staff are pasting customer records into a public generative AI tool to draft letters. What is the best first control?",
 "A policy with an approved AI tool", ["A ban on all AI use at work, unenforced", "Monitoring of staff browsing history", "A larger email storage quota"],
 "People use AI tools because they help. Giving them an approved tool with data protections, plus a clear policy on what may be entered, reduces leakage."),
]
